                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



    “A volunteer yelled at a donor. In January.” She paused. “It was a
lot.”
    “Can you turn it off? Just while I’m installing?”
    She looked genuinely sorry. “It’s on their app. It runs on a schedule.
It’s in the agreement, it plays while we’re open.” She touched my arm.
“Honestly, JC, it’s the nicest thing anyone’s done for this building in
years. Oh, and don’t be mad, the board is holding your milestone pay‐
ment until the partner review. It’s just process.”
    I said thank you. I don’t remember how. Rafa waited for me on the
sidewalk and didn’t say a word, and I loved him a little for that.
    Rent was due Thursday. I did the math on the sidewalk: a payment
on hold with no date, a favor I’d refused by email, a body that had been
awake for most of a week. That night I sold the synth I’d bought in a
burst of optimism and used twice, to a kid from Inwood who paid in
cash and said, “This thing is sick,” which it was, which was the prob‐
lem.



We ended at the café, where Mr. Rodríguez looked up from the register
and took in my face and Rafa’s shoes and reached under the counter
without a word for a stack of index cards and a marker.
   “You’ve been on a walk,” he said. “Write.”
   I wrote. Don Chino’s — flat. The barbershop — flat. Quiet. The Ballroom
— flat (new, January). I pinned them to the butcher-paper map with red
thumbtacks, where Jerome Avenue ran down the wall in pencil.
   “Three,” said Mr. Rodríguez. “How many more?”
   “Eleven,” said Rafa, “in six blocks.”
   We pinned them all. By the time we were done there was a red
cluster along the avenue, thicker on one side, with a thin line of tacks
running back toward our building. The map didn’t look like a story
anymore. It looked like an X-ray.
